review:
This game tries to be a lot of things but doesn't seem to do neither particularly well. One genre, that I particularly didn't appreciate, is an attempt at dating sim. I.E. there are certain customers you talk to, form bond and/or learn more about. It breaks the flow of the gameplay, it happens too often, not to mention these characters approach you along with the customers which makes you go on a lengthy dialogue when you least want it. There is no "skip the whole dialogue" option either.

The rest of the game is no better, while wide as an ocean in the beginning you will soon learn that it's deep as a puddle. 

- store customization is pointless, you get no rep from it apart from being a money sink and occasional quest goal;
- card collection is superficial, no tangible bonus from investing in the binder, can't even see the artwork in full-screen, I don't think;
- reputation mechanic is completely busted the only way to get reputation is from successful dialogue checks with customers, and you lose reputation from... well everything else... Mainly I was losing rep from failing dialogue checks, which I didn't care for in the first place;
- low rep leads to fewer customers, which slows down the gameplay loop, which makes everything more tedious;
- customers never buy more than one card, they won't leave the store without buying a card if you have some displayed, which makes their behavior seen simple and immersion breaking. Not to mention they don't care about the quality of the cards you sell, you can have only damaged, that won't change a thing;
- game coughed up an actual card-battle minigame around 3.5 hours in, but it's nothing special either..

Cute art style and dopamine stimulating sound-effects definitely make a good first impression, but in the long run, the game is shallow, short and forgettable.

Nice combat-oriented RPG. At the moment, the gameplay loop looks simple, but addictive: one or more battles in a row as part of the main quest or in additional modes, work with loot and, sometimes, communicating with the NPC.

+ 8 different classes / characters with unique skills;
+ interesting combat system;
+ good story;
+ - only one location (hub), battles take place on separate areas;
+ - no sidequests (non-plot battles are difficult to consider as such);
+ - RPG maker;
- not a very convenient interface.
I think that the game justifies its modest price!

[b][i]Let's Journey[/i][/b] was an extremely dull experience for me, and I could not last more than a few hours before I was completely and utterly bored with the game.

The main problem with this game is that the main gameplay loop in the game is extremely primitive and gets very monotonous and boring too quickly.

Given that I haven't played this game in a while, I don't know if any of these issues have been addressed, but given that I haven't seen many patch notes for this game since I last played, I believe I can safely say that this would be the case.

These are the issues that I found with the game:
- No keyboard support (can't even exit inventory with ESC, game require to click X with mouse);
- No volume settings (music is too loud and I can only turn it off completely);
- Lack of ingame info: can't see selected character bonuses/penalties, no tooltips;
- Very primitive and boring puzzles and minigame;
- Overall unsatisfying progression system: getting levels and spending points is way too generic;

[b]I cannot, in good conscience, recommend this game to anyone at this time.[/b]  Spend your money elsewhere.

I'm recommending this game since it looks promising. Yet, it needs TONS of improvements to become fun or at least playable.

Pros: great art, certain mechanics are fun, destroying humanity never gets old.

Cons:
- Confusing UI;
- Tedious resource management;
- Very slow at times (no way to speed up "ship activation" animations which mostly just repeat themselves);
- Unbalanced mechanics. Base upgrades don't really bring any change;
- Lots of missed opportunities: events are few and far between; Grays don't affect gameplay in any way; agents have no real use;
- Issues with English translation (the game is originally in Portuguese);
- BUGS bugs bugs bugs unfortunately not alien bugs but real ones.

There are bugs starting with tutorial (you may complete a step before you're instructed to and get into an Alt-F4 situation) and ending, um, with endgame. In 2020 you are forced to start an invasion, and after conquering one continent you're stuck. You cannot attack and you can be forever stuck with war power 0 in an endless battle. No save file to save you.

Great dock: lightweight app with lovely design! But there is still room for improvement:
1. Would be nice to have an option to always start Dennkind Dock in minimalistick mode;
2. Another useful thing will be an option to show/hide the dock not only with hotkey, but also hiding it automatically and showing it by placing a cursor near the screen border;
3. The library should have a more convenient editor that would allow changing icons and specifying launch parameters for applications;
4. I believe, grid sticking for desctop containers would be another good option;
5. Left/right screen border placement;
6. Prohibiting the launch of multiple copies of the dock application.

I wish further improvement to Dennkind Dock!

I haven't bought such a low-quality game for a long time:
- the gameplay is completely stolen from the FromSoftware games; Guys, can you at least come up with something of your own? *ashamed*
- graphic models cheap (few polygons);
- textures are cheaper;
- the game is not optimized (micro freezes);
